{"content": "<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Why Is the Sky Blue? — 为什么天空是蓝色的？</title>
<style>
  *,*::before,*::after{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0}

  :root{
    --sky-light:#e8f4fd;
    --sky-mid:#b8ddf0;
    --sky-deep:#1a5276;
    --sky-accent:#2e86c1;
    --text:#2c3e50;
    --text-light:#4a6a8a;
    --bg:#f7fbff;
    --card:#ffffff;
    --divider:#d0e4f0;
    --gold:#f0b429;
    --radius:10px;
  }

  html{scroll-behavior:smooth}
  body{
    font-family:'Segoe UI','PingFang SC','Microsoft YaHei','Noto Sans SC',system-ui,sans-serif;
    color:var(--text);
    background:linear-gradient(160deg,var(--bg) 0%,var(--sky-light) 50%,var(--sky-mid) 100%);
    min-height:100vh;
    line-height:1.7;
  }

  /* ── Header ── */
  header{
    text-align:center;
    padding:2.5rem 1rem 1.5rem;
    background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a5276 0%,#2e86c1 60%,#5dade2 100%);
    color:#fff;
    position:relative;
    overflow:hidden;
  }
  header::after{
    content:'';
    position:absolute;bottom:-2px;left:0;right:0;height:32px;
    background:var(--bg);
    border-radius:50% 50% 0 0 / 100% 100% 0 0;
  }
  header h1{font-size:clamp(1.5rem,4vw,2.4rem);font-weight:700;margin-bottom:.25rem;text-shadow:0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.25)}
  header h1 span{font-weight:300;opacity:.85;margin:0 .2rem}
  header p.sub{font-size:1rem;opacity:.85;font-weight:300}

  /* ── Controls ── */
  .controls{
    display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:.5rem;
    padding:1rem 1rem .5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;
  }
  .controls button{
    padding:.45rem 1.1rem;
    border:2px solid var(--sky-accent);
    background:transparent;color:var(--sky-accent);
    border-radius:999px;font-size:.85rem;font-weight:600;
    cursor:pointer;transition:all .2s;
  }
  .controls button:hover,.controls button.active{
    background:var(--sky-accent);color:#fff;
  }

  /* ── Main grid ── */
  main{
    max-width:1440px;margin:0 auto;
    padding:1rem 1rem 3rem;
  }

  .grid{
    display:grid;
    grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;
    gap:1.5rem;
    align-items:start;
  }
  .grid.single-en{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .grid.single-cn{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  .grid.single-en .col-cn,.grid.single-cn .col-en{display:none}

  .col-en .panel,.col-cn .panel{display:block}
  .grid.single-en .col-cn .panel,.grid.single-cn .col-en .panel{display:none}

  /* ── Panel card ── */
  .panel{
    background:var(--card);
    border-radius:var(--radius);
    box-shadow:0 2px 16px rgba(26,82,118,.08);
    padding:1.8rem 2rem;
    border:1px solid var(--divider);
    transition:box-shadow .3s;
  }
  .panel:hover{box-shadow:0 4px 24px rgba(26,82,118,.13)}

  .panel h2{
    font-size:1.35rem;color:var(--sky-deep);
    border-bottom:2px solid var(--sky-mid);
    padding-bottom:.4rem;margin-bottom:1rem;
  }
  .panel h3{
    font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--sky-accent);
    margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem;
  }
  .panel p{margin-bottom:.8rem}
  .panel strong{color:var(--sky-deep)}
  .panel hr{
    border:none;height:1px;margin:1.5rem 0;
    background:linear-gradient(90deg,transparent,var(--divider),transparent);
  }
  .panel ol{padding-left:1.4rem;margin-bottom:.8rem}
  .panel ol li{margin-bottom:.35rem}

  /* ── Math blocks ── */
  .math{
    display:block;
    text-align:center;
    padding:.7rem 1rem;margin:.7rem 0;
    background:var(--sky-light);
    border-radius:8px;font-size:1.05rem;
    font-family:'Cambria Math','Times New Roman',serif;
    letter-spacing:.5px;color:var(--sky-deep);
    border-left:3px solid var(--sky-accent);
    overflow-x:auto;
  }
  .math-inline{
    font-family:'Cambria Math','Times New Roman',serif;
    color:var(--sky-deep);font-weight:600;
    background:rgba(46,134,193,.08);
    padding:.1em .35em;border-radius:4px;
    white-space:nowrap;
  }

  /* ── Footer ── */
  footer{
    text-align:center;padding:1rem;
    color:var(--text-light);font-size:.8rem;opacity:.7;
  }

  /* ── Responsive ── */
  @media(max-width:900px){
    .grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
    .grid.single-en .col-cn,.grid.single-cn .col-en{display:block}
    .grid.single-en .col-cn .panel,.grid.single-cn .col-en .panel{display:none}
    .panel{padding:1.3rem 1.4rem}
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ════════ HEADER ════════ -->
<header>
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?<span>·</span>为什么天空是蓝色的？</h1>
  <p class="sub">Three explanations for three audiences · 三种解释，三种受众</p>
</header>

<!-- ════════ CONTROLS ════════ -->
<div class="controls">
  <button class="active" onclick="show('both')">🌐 Both / 双语</button>
  <button onclick="show('en')">🇬🇧 English Only</button>
  <button onclick="show('cn')">🇨🇳 仅中文</button>
</div>

<!-- ════════ CONTENT ════════ -->
<main>
<div class="grid" id="grid">

  <!-- ──── ENGLISH COLUMN ──── -->
  <div class="col-en">
    <div class="panel">

      <h2>🧒 For a 3-Year-Old</h2>
      <p>When the Sun shines on us, its light looks white, but it's actually hiding ALL the colors of a rainbow inside it! As sunlight travels through the big blanket of air around our Earth, the blue colors bounce off tiny floating things in the air — kind of how you bounce a ball! Blue bounces around the most, so when you look up, you see all that bouncy blue light coming at your eyes. That's why the sky is blue! ☀️💙</p>
      <p><em>(At sunset, the sunlight has to travel much further through the air, so the blue gets bounced away completely and you're left with pretty reds and oranges instead.)</em></p>

      <hr>

      <h2>🎓 For a High School Student</h2>
      <p>The sky is blue due to a phenomenon called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>. Sunlight appears white, but it's actually made of all visible wavelengths — from red (long wavelengths, ~700 nm) to violet (short wavelengths, ~400 nm).</p>
      <p>When sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with gas molecules (mostly nitrogen and oxygen). These molecules are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light. When light encounters particles significantly smaller than its wavelength, shorter wavelengths are scattered much more strongly than longer ones. Specifically, Rayleigh scattering intensity is proportional to <span class="math-inline">1/λ⁴</span>, meaning blue light (~450 nm) is scattered roughly <strong>9–10× more</strong> than red light (~650 nm).</p>
      <p>This scattered blue light is redirected in all directions, so no matter where you look in the sky, blue light reaches your eyes. That's why the whole sky appears blue.</p>
      <p><strong>What about violet?</strong> Violet light has an even shorter wavelength and scatters even more strongly. However, the sky doesn't look violet because:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The Sun emits less violet light than blue light.</li>
        <li>Our eyes are far more sensitive to blue than violet.</li>
        <li>Some violet light is absorbed by the upper atmosphere.</li>
      </ol>

      <hr>

      <h2>🔬 For a Physics PhD</h2>
      <p>The blue color of the sky arises from <strong>elastic scattering of sunlight by atmospheric molecules</strong>, described by Rayleigh scattering in the limit where the scattering particles are small relative to the wavelength of incident radiation (<span class="math-inline">ka ≪ 1</span>).</p>

      <h3>Electromagnetic Origin</h3>
      <p>An incident electromagnetic wave induces an oscillating dipole moment in polarizable molecules. The electric field <strong>E</strong> drives the electron cloud:</p>
      <span class="math"><strong>p</strong> = α<strong>E</strong>₀ e<sup>−iωt</sup></span>
      <p>where α is the molecular polarizability. The re-radiated power per solid angle from an oscillating dipole is:</p>
      <span class="math">dP/dΩ = (p²ω⁴ / 16π²ε₀c³) · (1 + cos²θ)</span>
      <p>Since ω = 2πc/λ, the total scattered intensity scales as:</p>
      <span class="math">I<sub>scattered</sub> ∝ 1 / λ⁴</span>
      <p>This <span class="math-inline">λ⁻⁴ dependence</span> is the core of Rayleigh's result and explains the strong preferential scattering of shorter wavelengths.</p>

      <h3>Quantum Mechanical Perspective</h3>
      <p>More rigorously, this can be framed as <strong>elastic (Resonance) Raman scattering</strong> in the zero-phonon limit. For N₂ and O₂ (the dominant atmospheric constituents), the relevant electronic transitions lie in the UV (~100 nm for N₂), well off-resonance from visible light. In this far-detuned regime, the scattering cross-section from second-order perturbation theory is:</p>
      <span class="math">σ<sub>scatt</sub> = (8π/3) · k⁴ · | Σ<sub>n</sub> ⟨g|<b>r</b>|n⟩·⟨n|<b>r</b>|g⟩ / (E<sub>n</sub> − E<sub>g</sub> − ℏω) |²</span>
      <p>The denominator ensures the λ⁻⁴ dependence when ω is small compared to the nearest resonance frequency. Additionally, anisotropic polarizability of diatomic molecules introduces a <strong>depolarization ratio</strong> of ~0.027–0.036 for N₂ and O₂, meaning the scattered light is not perfectly polarized.</p>

      <h3>Why Not Violet?</h3>
      <p>While violet (~400 nm) scatters ~2.4× more than blue (~450 nm) by the λ⁻⁴ law, the observed sky color is determined by the <strong>convolution</strong> of three factors:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Solar spectral irradiance</strong> — AM1.5 drops significantly in the violet/UV.</li>
        <li><strong>Atmospheric transmission</strong> — O₃ absorption (Hartley and Huggins bands) attenuates much of the violet/UV.</li>
        <li><strong>Human photopic response</strong> — V(λ) peaks at ~555 nm; the S-cone (short-wavelength) sensitivity at 400 nm is a fraction of its sensitivity at 450 nm.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The net effect is that the sky's spectral radiance peaks in the blue, not the violet.</p>

      <h3>Angular Dependence</h3>
      <p>The <span class="math-inline">1 + cos²θ</span> angular distribution means forward and backward scattering are equal, with a minimum at 90°. At 90° from the Sun, the scattered light is maximally <strong>polarized</strong> — a phenomenon you can observe with polarizing sunglasses, which will darken the sky most at right angles to the Sun.</p>

      <h3>Beyond Rayleigh</h3>
      <p>At very short wavelengths or for aerosol/larger particle contributions (Mie scattering, where <span class="math-inline">ka ≳ 1</span>), the scattering becomes more isotropic and less wavelength-dependent, which is why haze and pollution tend to whiten the sky rather than deepen its blue.</p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ──── CHINESE COLUMN ──── -->
  <div class="col-cn">
    <div class="panel">

      <h2>🧒 给3岁小朋友</h2>
      <p>当太阳照在我们身上时，它的阳光看起来是白色的，但其实里面藏着彩虹的所有颜色！阳光穿过包裹地球的那层厚厚的空气时，蓝色会撞到空气中漂浮的小颗粒然后弹开——就像你拍皮球一样！蓝色弹得最多，所以当你抬头看时，就会看到到处都是弹过来的蓝色光。这就是天空是蓝色的原因！☀️💙</p>
      <p><em>（日落的时候，阳光要穿过更多的空气才能到达你的眼睛，所以蓝色全被弹走了，剩下的就是漂亮的红色和橙色啦。）</em></p>

      <hr>

      <h2>🎓 给高中生</h2>
      <p>天空呈现蓝色是由一种叫做<strong>瑞利散射</strong>的现象造成的。太阳光看起来是白色的，但实际上它由所有可见光波长组成——从红光（长波长，约700纳米）到紫光（短波长，约400纳米）。</p>
      <p>当阳光进入地球大气层时，它会与气体分子（主要是氮气和氧气）发生碰撞。这些分子的尺寸远小于可见光的波长。当光遇到远小于其波长的颗粒时，短波长的光会被散射得比长波长的光强烈得多。具体来说，瑞利散射的强度与<span class="math-inline">1/λ⁴</span>成正比，这意味着蓝光（约450纳米）被散射的程度大约是红光（约650纳米）的<strong>9–10倍</strong>。</p>
      <p>这些被散射的蓝光向各个方向传播，所以无论你看天空的哪个方向，蓝光都会进入你的眼睛。这就是整片天空看起来是蓝色的原因。</p>
      <p><strong>那紫光呢？</strong> 紫光的波长更短，散射更强。然而天空并不呈现紫色，原因是：</p>
      <ol>
        <li>太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少。</li>
        <li>人眼对蓝色的敏感度远高于紫色。</li>
        <li>部分紫光被高层大气吸收掉了。</li>
      </ol>

      <hr>

      <h2>🔬 给物理学博士</h2>
      <p>天空的蓝色源于<strong>大气分子对日光的弹性散射</strong>，在散射粒子远小于入射辐射波长的极限下由瑞利散射描述（<span class="math-inline">ka ≪ 1</span>）。</p>

      <h3>电磁学起源</h3>
      <p>入射电磁波在可极化分子中感应出振荡偶极矩。电场<strong>E</strong>驱动电子云：</p>
      <span class="math"><strong>p</strong> = α<strong>E</strong>₀ e<sup>−iωt</sup></span>
      <p>其中 α 为分子极化率。振荡偶极子每单位立体角再辐射的功率为：</p>
      <span class="math">dP/dΩ = (p²ω⁴ / 16π²ε₀c³) · (1 + cos²θ)</span>
      <p>由于 ω = 2πc/λ，总散射强度按以下方式缩放：</p>
      <span class="math">I<sub>scattered</sub> ∝ 1 / λ⁴</span>
      <p>这种<span class="math-inline">λ⁻⁴依赖关系</span>是瑞利结果的核心，解释了短波长光被强烈优先散射的原因。</p>

      <h3>量子力学视角</h3>
      <p>更严格地说，这可以表述为零声子极限下的<strong>弹性（共振）拉曼散射</strong>。对于 N₂ 和 O₂（大气的主要成分），相关的电子跃迁位于紫外波段（N₂ 约100纳米），远离可见光的共振。在这种远失谐 regime 下，由二阶微扰理论得到的散射截面为：</p>
      <span class="math">σ<sub>scatt</sub> = (8π/3) · k⁴ · | Σ<sub>n</sub> ⟨g|<b>r</b>|n⟩·⟨n|<b>r</b>|g⟩ / (E<sub>n</sub> − E<sub>g</sub> − ℏω) |²</span>
      <p>分母保证了当 ω 远小于最近共振频率时出现 λ⁻⁴ 依赖关系。此外，双原子分子的非各向同性极化率引入了约 <strong>0.027–0.036</strong> 的<strong>退偏比</strong>（对 N₂ 和 O₂），意味着散射光并非完全偏振。</p>

      <h3>为什么不是紫色？</h3>
      <p>尽管根据 λ⁻⁴ 定律，紫光（约400纳米）的散射强度比蓝光（约450纳米）强约 <strong>2.4倍</strong>，但观测到的天空颜色由以下三个因素的<strong>卷积</strong>决定：</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>太阳光谱辐照度</strong> — AM1.5 标准在紫/紫外波段显著下降。</li>
        <li><strong>大气透过率</strong> — O₃ 吸收（哈特里带和哈金斯带）衰减了大量紫/紫外光。</li>
        <li><strong>人眼光视响应</strong> — V(λ) 在约555纳米处达到峰值；S视锥细胞（短波长）在400纳米的灵敏度仅为450纳米处的一小部分。</li>
      </ol>
      <p>综合效果是天空的光谱辐亮度峰值出现在蓝色而非紫色。</p>

      <h3>角度依赖性</h3>
      <p><span class="math-inline">1 + cos²θ</span> 的角度分布意味着前向散射和后向散射相等，在90°处达到最小值。在与太阳成90°的方向上，散射光达到最大<strong>偏振</strong>——这一现象可以通过偏振太阳镜观察到，在与太阳成直角的方向上天空会变暗最明显。</p>

      <h3>超越瑞利散射</h3>
      <p>在极短波长或气溶胶/较大粒子的贡献下（米氏散射，其中 <span class="math-inline">ka ≳ 1</span>），散射变得更具各向同性且对波长的依赖性减弱，这就是为什么雾霾和污染会使天空变白而非加深蓝色的原因。</p>

    </div>
  </div>

</div>
</main>

<footer>Bilingual Sky Presentation · 双语天空演示</footer>

<script>
  function show(mode) {
    const grid = document.getElementById('grid');
    grid.classList.remove('single-en', 'single-cn');
    if (mode === 'en') grid.classList.add('single-en');
    else if (mode === 'cn') grid.classList.add('single-cn');

    document.querySelectorAll('.controls button').forEach(btn => btn.classList.remove('active'));
    event.target.classList.add('active');
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>", "path": "/home/agent/workspace/index.html"}

